<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Jan 6, 2023">
<CPscript title="Conformal mapping of multiply connected plane regions" >
<text> This script illustrates the basics for the conformal mapping of multiply connected plane regions onto standard domains, namely, 'circle' domain (those whose boundary components are circles). </text>
<Section title="Packing the plane domain">
<text> The packing of a plane region is created by 'cookie-cutting' from an initial packing. In this illustration, we create a large hex packing, then remove parts by cutting along prepared curves. </text>
<cmd iconname="userconfig.png">act 0;seed -q;geom_to_e;add_gen 30 6;set_rad .02 a;layout;disp -w -c;infile_path test_curve.g;disp -g; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="metacontact_online.png">cookie;disp -w -c -g; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="icq_dnd.png">disp -w -g;infile_path dc_1.g;cookie;disp -c -g; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="format_increaseindent.png">disp -w -g;infile_path dc_2.g;cookie;disp -c -g; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="editdelete.png">disp -w -g;infile_path dc_3.g;cookie;disp -c -g;copy 1; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Packing the unit disc">
<text> An 'ideal' vertex is created for each of the bounded boundary components and connected to each of the vertices defining the boundary of that component. I've created an artificial geometric image here to suggest the result, but in fact all we get from this stage is the combinatorics. </text>
<cmd iconname="bookmark_add.png">act 0;mark -cw -c b(446 446);add_ideal 446;set_center -.5 .25 M;mark -c m M;disp -e mv;mark -cw -c b(760 760);add_ideal 760;set_center .21 -.46 M;mark -c m M;disp -e mv;mark -cw -c b(429 429);add_ideal 429;set_center .27 .58 M;mark -c m M;disp -e mv; </cmd>
<text> We 'max' pack this new complex in the unit disc; the computation will take a few seconds. </text>
<cmd iconname="list.png">act 0;set_cycles 30000;max_pack;disp -w -c; </cmd>
<text> We need to convert this packing to euclidean geometry and remove the three 'ideal' circles that form the holes. Before getting rid of them, however, we record their hyperbolic centers and radii in the 'Msg/Scratch' pane -- these represent the modulus of the original region (at least, a 'discrete' version of the modulus). </text>
<cmd iconname="debugger.png">act 0;?center M;?rad M;puncture M;?center M;?rad M;puncture M;?center M;?rad M;puncture M;geom_to_e;disp -w -c;disp -p1 -w -c; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Investigating the result">
<text> Placing the domain and range side by side, one can investigate the map between the resulting packings. We remove the temporary ideal circles that were added earlier, then clicking the left mouse button on a circle in one packing (or the middle mouse button on a face) will highlight it in both packings. </text>
<cmd iconname="delete.png">Map 0 1;disp -p1 -w -c; </cmd>
<text> Lastly, we color code the faces in both packings according to the quasiconformal distortion under the correspondence: namely, a face is blue if the dilatation for the barycentric map on that face exceeds 1.1 and progressively darker red if the dilatation is between 1 and 1.1. </text>
<cmd iconname="network.png">act 0;color -f qc -q 1 -m 1.15;color -p1 -f qc -q 0 -m 1.15;disp -w -fcf -u;disp -p1 -w -fcf ; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Closing note">
<text> If a more accurate estimate of the mapping or of the modulus is desired, once can use finer packings in this same process. The only changes needed are in the first command (creating the packing) and in the command which adds the ideal vertices, where one must provide the indices of vertices on the boundaries of the three bounded components. </text>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
<CPdata>
